meta {
  name: 07 SnapTrade (direct)
  seq: 7
}

script:pre-request {
  // SnapTrade signs every request. The signature is an HMAC-SHA256 over a
  // canonical JSON object {content, path, query} — compact separators, sorted
  // keys — keyed with the consumer key, base64-encoded. It then rides in the
  // `Signature` header while clientId and timestamp travel as query params.
  //
  // This runs for every request in this folder, so individual requests only
  // declare their own params. Mirrors compute_request_signature() in the
  // SnapTrade SDK (snaptrade_client/request_after_hook.py).
  //
  // Bruno's sandbox allows require() only for whitelisted modules, so this
  // pulls in 'crypto-js' (whitelisted) rather than Node's 'crypto' (not), and
  // there is no `URL` constructor — hence the string parsing below. The
  // crypto-js HMAC was verified byte-identical to Node's, and this script was
  // verified against the live API for both a GET and a POST-with-body.
  const CryptoJS = require('crypto-js');

  const clientId = bru.getEnvVar('snaptradeClientId');
  const consumerKey = bru.getEnvVar('snaptradeConsumerKey');

  if (!clientId || !consumerKey) {
    throw new Error(
      'Set snaptradeClientId and snaptradeConsumerKey in the Local.private ' +
      'Bruno environment (copy them from backend/.env).'
    );
  }

  // bru.interpolate is essential here, not a convenience. Bruno expands
  // {{vars}} *after* this script runs (interpolateVars is called downstream of
  // the pre-request hook), and req.getUrl() returns the URL raw. Signing the
  // un-expanded template would hash "{{snaptradeBaseUrl}}/accounts" while
  // sending the real URL, and SnapTrade rejects it:
  //   401 {"detail":"Unable to verify signature sent","code":"1076"}
  // Interpolating here means the string we sign is the string that is sent.
  const rawUrl = bru.interpolate(req.getUrl());
  const hashIdx = rawUrl.indexOf('#');
  const noHash = hashIdx === -1 ? rawUrl : rawUrl.slice(0, hashIdx);
  const qIdx = noHash.indexOf('?');
  const base = qIdx === -1 ? noHash : noHash.slice(0, qIdx);
  const rawQuery = qIdx === -1 ? '' : noHash.slice(qIdx + 1);

  // Keep the request's own params, dropping any stale auth params so a replay
  // cannot sign two different timestamps.
  const params = [];
  if (rawQuery) {
    rawQuery.split('&').forEach(function (pair) {
      if (!pair) return;
      const eq = pair.indexOf('=');
      const key = eq === -1 ? pair : pair.slice(0, eq);
      const value = eq === -1 ? '' : pair.slice(eq + 1);
      if (key !== 'clientId' && key !== 'timestamp') {
        params.push([key, value]);
      }
    });
  }

  params.push(['clientId', encodeURIComponent(clientId)]);
  params.push(['timestamp', String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000))]);

  // Sort by key: the signature covers the query string verbatim, so the order
  // signed here has to be the order sent.
  params.sort(function (a, b) {
    return a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : a[0] > b[0] ? 1 : 0;
  });

  const query = params
    .map(function (pair) {
      return pair[0] + '=' + pair[1];
    })
    .join('&');

  // Path only — the signature excludes scheme and host.
  const schemeEnd = base.indexOf('://');
  const afterScheme = schemeEnd === -1 ? base : base.slice(schemeEnd + 3);
  const firstSlash = afterScheme.indexOf('/');
  const path = firstSlash === -1 ? '/' : afterScheme.slice(firstSlash);

  // An absent or empty body signs as null, matching the SDK. Interpolated for
  // the same reason as the URL: a {{var}} in the body would otherwise be
  // expanded after signing.
  let body = req.getBody();
  if (body === undefined || body === null) {
    body = null;
  } else {
    if (typeof body === 'string') {
      body = bru.interpolate(body);
      try {
        body = JSON.parse(body);
      } catch (e) {
        // Not JSON — sign the string as-is.
      }
    } else if (typeof body === 'object') {
      body = bru.interpolate(body);
    }
    if (typeof body === 'object' && body !== null && Object.keys(body).length === 0) {
      body = null;
    }
  }

  // The three keys are already alphabetical, so this matches the SDK's
  // sort_keys=True canonical form.
  const sigContent = JSON.stringify({ content: body, path: path, query: query });

  const signature = CryptoJS.HmacSHA256(sigContent, consumerKey).toString(
    CryptoJS.enc.Base64
  );

  req.setUrl(base + '?' + query);
  req.setHeader('Signature', signature);
  req.setHeader('Accept', 'application/json');
}

docs {
  # SnapTrade — direct API calls

  These requests hit **SnapTrade's API directly**, not the Achalara backend.

  Achalara has no SnapTrade routes yet (they arrive with the connections
  service and webhook handler), so there is nothing local to call. Once those
  land, this folder stays useful for comparing our responses against the
  upstream source.

  ## Setup

  Select the **Local.private** environment. It is gitignored and holds the real
  credentials; `Local` is the tracked, placeholder-only env.

  This split exists because **Bruno writes secret var values inline into the
  environment file** — listing a var under `vars:secret` does not keep its
  value out of the file. Since this repo is public, any env holding real
  credentials has to stay untracked. If you add credentials, put them in
  `Local.private.bru`, never in `Local.bru`.

  Vars the requests use (copy credential values from `backend/.env`):

  ```
  snaptradeBaseUrl:     https://api.snaptrade.com
  snaptradeClientId:    <SNAPTRADE_CLIENT_ID>
  snaptradeConsumerKey: <SNAPTRADE_CONSUMER_KEY>
  snaptradeUserId:      achalara-local-test-user   (must match the linked user)
  snaptradeAccountId:   the account to inspect — set it yourself, from
                        "List Accounts". Nothing overwrites it.
  snaptradeConnectionId: from "List Connections" — an authorization id, not an
                         account id; only Refresh/Delete need it
  ```

  No request writes to the environment. Every id is yours to set, so running
  one request never silently changes what another one targets.

  ## Auth

  Handled by this folder's pre-request script — it computes the `Signature`
  header and injects `clientId` + `timestamp`. Individual requests declare
  only their own params. Credentials never appear in a request file.

  ## Rate limits

  - **250 requests/minute** per clientId (customer-level).
  - **10 requests/minute per account** — enforced for Personal keys. Reading
    balances and reading positions share one bucket per account.
  - Transactions: SnapTrade asks for **>= 24h between calls per account**.

  Click deliberately; don't hammer these. A 429 returns `Retry-After`.

  ## Read-only

  Nothing here places an order. The connection was created with
  `connectionType=read`, so trading endpoints are not authorized.
}
